Figure  11  illustrates the variation in crack length versus number of cycles for both BM and AW specimens before and after service under different loading angles. The results indicate that for a given material type, residual fatigue life increases with the loading angle. Possible explanations for this trend have been discussed in detail in the studies by Zhang et al. [ 3 ] and Kannengießer et al. [ 52 ]. In addition, a comparison between pre-service and post-service BM and AW specimens reveals a general reduction in remaining useful life after service exposure. To quantify the degree of life degradation, a life reduction rate was calculated using the formula shown in Eq. ( 4 ). The results show that service-induced cyclic loading had a relatively minor impact on the FCG resistance of BM specimens, with a maximum life reduction rate of 22.31% observed at a 45° loading angle. In contrast, the cyclic loads experienced during service significantly weakened the crack growth resistance of AW specimens, with a maximum life reduction rate of 70.54% at a 60° loading angle. These findings suggest that accumulated fatigue damage has a much greater impact on AW than on BM. This is mainly attributed to the unavoidable presence of microscopic defects in welds, such as microcracks, inclusions, and porosity [ 11 ,  53 ]. Under cyclic loading, these defects act as stress concentrators and preferential sites for fatigue crack initiation, thereby accelerating damage accumulation and degrading the fatigue performance of welded joints. 4 λ = N Pre-service - N Post-service N Pre-service , \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
